<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carpathian Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carpathians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carpathian Mountains form an arc about 1,500 km long across central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. They enclose the Pannonian Basin, which the Hungarians entered across their passes in 895. The range has long been a frontier between peoples and states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains.jpg" alt="Carpathian Mountains" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakub Krajňák · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morskie_Oko_in_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 568 the Lombards left Pannonia for Italy, and the Avars took over the whole basin, the great plain enclosed by the <b>Carpathians</b> and the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wallachia lies north of the Lower Danube and south of the Southern <b>Carpathians</b>, divided by the Olt River into Muntenia in the east and Oltenia in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through colonisation and acculturation the formative Pre-Cucuteni/Trypillia A culture took shape, and during the 5th millennium BCE it spread from its homeland between the Prut and the Siret along the eastern foothills of the <b>Carpathians</b> to the plains of the Dnieper and Southern Bug.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carpi lived in Moldavia, east of the <b>Carpathians</b> and outside Roman Dacia, from the 2nd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a></li><li><blockquote>In 895 Pechenegs and Bulgarians attacked Etelköz, and the Hungarians crossed the <b>Carpathians</b> into the basin, founding the Principality of Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-tribal-federation">Hungarian tribal federation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Pecheneg attacks drove them from Etelköz, the Hungarian tribal federation under Árpád crossed the <b>Carpathians</b> in 895.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
